{"answer":"Lifecycle memory strength control is directly addressed by StageMem’s explicit lifecycle state “strength” for retention consequence, which treats post-admission failure as a lifecycle-control problem and distinguishes it from relying only on indirect signals like validity, similarity, recency, frequency, importance, or summarization. It exposes promotion, compression, and eviction pressure points via staged stores, aiming to avoid failures such as retaining the wrong high-cue content or forgetting costly premises. [1]

Other papers relate to lifecycle control but focus on different mechanisms. Oblivion controls memory accessibility via decay-driven activation while decoupling read and write decisions, reinforcing memories contributing to the response rather than introducing an explicit “strength” retention state. [2] Dual-Layer Agentic Memory manages the knowledge lifecycle by selective externalization and periodic internalization through parametric consolidation. [9] MARS maintains explicit strength and evidence tracking within preference memory and schedules a six-operation lifecycle for recommendation, but in a recommendation/personalization setting. [8]

If you meant the general idea of lifecycle-aware governance (not specifically an explicit “strength” state), the survey frames writing/offloading/eviction/consolidation/forgetting as state transitions in an information lifecycle and argues for dynamic, governable, lifecycle-aware memory management. [6]", "grounded": true}
